(2307121, 318856, 30049, '<td valign="top">Sorry, don''t see the connection to the challenge. ???  As for the photo, it''s exciting to have an opportunity to get this kind of image.  Looks like your camera was having a hard time getting focus past the branches as the branches are detailed but the owl is a little OOF.  Key is getting the face and eyes sharp.  Subject is also a bit centered.  Good luck.</td>', 'Sorry, don''t see the connection to the challenge. ???  As for the photo, it''s exciting to have an opportunity to get this kind of image.  Looks like your camera was having a hard time getting focus past the branches as the branches are detailed but the owl is a little OOF.  Key is getting the face and eyes sharp.  Subject is also a bit centered.  Good luck.', '2006-04-12 14:07:50.000000', NULL, 1),
(2307126, 318749, 7322, '<td valign="top">I''m not sure this picture is really capturing much of great interest. I''m not getting much in the way of expression or mood. It just seems to be a snap of the child as he walks in front of you. I understand the idea was to show the street, and give the sense of the ''long walk'' but this just isn''t doing it. For one thing, there are houses not that far away and home could be one of those as far as the viewer knows. The other problem is there isn''t really anything we can see from this rear-view that gives the viewer an idea of the boy''s emotions. One of way of capturing the feeling it looks like you were after is to shoot from a much lower viewpoint, either at the child''s eyelevel, or even lower. This would have the two-fold effect (hopefully) of making the road appear more looming, and giving us more of an impression of the boy''s experience because we are viewing everything from his eye level. If you shoot from even lower, it will give more of the feeling of a child looking up at the world of grown-ups.</td>', 'I''m not sure this picture is really capturing much of great interest. I''m not getting much in the way of expression or mood. It just seems to be a snap of the child as he walks in front of you. I understand the idea was to show the street, and give the sense of the ''long walk'' but this just isn''t doing it. For one thing, there are houses not that far away and home could be one of those as far as the viewer knows. The other problem is there isn''t really anything we can see from this rear-view that gives the viewer an idea of the boy''s emotions. One of way of capturing the feeling it looks like you were after is to shoot from a much lower viewpoint, either at the child''s eyelevel, or even lower. This would have the two-fold effect (hopefully) of making the road appear more looming, and giving us more of an impression of the boy''s experience because we are viewing everything from his eye level. If you shoot from even lower, it will give more of the feeling of a child looking up at the world of grown-ups.', '2006-04-12 14:08:44.000000', NULL, 1),
(2307130, 319964, 44780, '<td valign="top">Love it!</td>', 'Love it!', '2006-04-12 14:09:23.000000', NULL, 1),
